## The Numbers Behind the Alarming Trend

Did you know that 2026 has already seen 419 food recalls? And among these, 103 have been classified as Class I recalls, indicating a serious risk to public health. For comparison, the total number of recalls recorded in history stands at 777, meaning this year alone accounts for over half of the recalls of all time.

### A Look at Repeat Offenders

A staggering 74 companies have been flagged as repeat offenders this year. Walking the hall of infamy, Moonlight Packing Corporation and Albertsons Companies LLC have both garnered 11 recalls, 10 and 11 of which, respectively, were Class I. This raises questions about the effectiveness of regulatory measures and internal quality controls.

## The Biggest Offenders

Moonlight Packing Corporation scores the lowest in our rankings with a dismal 96% recall score, earning them an F grade. Albertsons follows close behind with a score of 94%. Both have faced numerous recalls for Class I issues, such as the presence of undeclared allergens. With food safety being critical, the alarming performance of these companies calls for immediate action.

### Recent Events

The most recent recalls, dated May 13, 2026, include cases such as Silver Moon LP dba Loard's Ice Cream recalling several ice cream variants for undeclared allergens, and California Dairies Inc. recalling dried milk powder for potential Salmonella contamination. These incidents serve as stark reminders of the hidden dangers lurking in our food supply.

## What Can Be Done?

This year's numbers and repeat offenders underline the dire need for stricter food safety regulations and enforcement. Consumers should remain vigilant, always verify the safety of their purchases, and report any issues. But more importantly, systemic reforms are needed to hold companies accountable and prevent such incidents from recurring.
